#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711320 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(14,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{y} + \\sqrt{x + 2}=6$.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate the square roots using the power and chain rules.\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\sqrt{y} + \\sqrt{x + 2}\\right)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(6\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{\\frac{dy}{dx}}{2 \\sqrt{y}} + \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{x + 2}}&=0\\\\\n\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{y}}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{x + 2}}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{x + 2}}}{\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{y}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(14,4)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $\\frac{1}{4}\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(14,4)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\frac{1}{8}}{\\frac{1}{4}}\\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711323 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(11,16)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{y} + \\sqrt{x - 2}=7$.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate the square roots using the power and chain rules.\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\sqrt{y} + \\sqrt{x - 2}\\right)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(7\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{\\frac{dy}{dx}}{2 \\sqrt{y}} + \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{x - 2}}&=0\\\\\n\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{y}}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{x - 2}}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{x - 2}}}{\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{y}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(11,16)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $\\frac{1}{8}\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(11,16)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\frac{1}{6}}{\\frac{1}{8}}\\\\\n&=- \\frac{4}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711326 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(- \\frac{\\pi}{6},1)$ for the curve $y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Use the product rule on the cosine product; the derivative of $\\cos(x)$ is $-\\sin(x)$.\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}&=0\\\\\n\\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(- y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{- y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(- \\frac{\\pi}{6},1)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(- \\frac{\\pi}{6},1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711336 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(-2,1)$ for the curve $x^{2} y + x=2$.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Use the product rule on the mixed power term and the chain rule on powers of $y$.\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(x^{2} y + x\\right)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} x^{2} + 2 x y + 1&=0\\\\\n\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(2 x y + 1\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{2 x y + 1}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(-2,1)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $4\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(-2,1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{-3}{4}\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711337 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(-2,0)$ for the curve $2 x^{2} - 2 x y - 2 y^{2}=8$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Use the product rule on $xy$ and the chain rule on powers of $y$.\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(2 x^{2} - 2 x y - 2 y^{2}\\right)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(8\\right)\\\\\n- 2 \\frac{dy}{dx} x - 4 \\frac{dy}{dx} y + 4 x - 2 y&=0\\\\\n\\left(- 2 x - 4 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(4 x - 2 y\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{4 x - 2 y}{- 2 x - 4 y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(-2,0)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $4\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(-2,0)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{-8}{4}\\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711351 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - y^{2}=- x e^{y}$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(2 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=- 2 x^{2} y' \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 4 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- x e^{y}\\right)&=- x y' e^{y} - e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + y' \\left(- 2 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 y\\right)&=- x y' e^{y}\\\\\n&\\quad -e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(- 2 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + x e^{y} - 2 y\\right)y^{\\prime}&=- 4 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad -e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{- 4 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - e^{y}}{- 2 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + x e^{y} - 2 y}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $- 2 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + x e^{y} - 2 y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711357 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 y^{2}=x e^{y}$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=- 3 x^{2} y' \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 6 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x e^{y}\\right)&=x y' e^{y} + e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + y' \\left(- 3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 4 y\\right)&=x y' e^{y}\\\\\n&\\quad +e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(- 3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - x e^{y} + 4 y\\right)y^{\\prime}&=- 6 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad +e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{- 6 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + e^{y}}{- 3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - x e^{y} + 4 y}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $- 3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - x e^{y} + 4 y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711362 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $3 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y^{2}=- x^{2} e^{y} - 2$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=3 x y' \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(- x^{2} e^{y}\\right)&=- x^{2} y' e^{y} - 2 x e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny' \\left(3 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 6 y\\right) + 3 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}&=- 2 x e^{y}\\\\\n&\\quad -x^{2} y' e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x^{2} e^{y} + 3 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 6 y\\right)y^{\\prime}&=- 2 x e^{y}\\\\\n&\\quad -3 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{- 2 x e^{y} - 3 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}{x^{2} e^{y} + 3 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 6 y}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $x^{2} e^{y} + 3 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 6 y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711369 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for $3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 3 y^{2}=2 x e^{y} + 1$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$ and write $y'=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$. Use the **product rule** for each mixed product, including the chain-rule factor $y^{\\prime}$ when differentiating a function of $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=3 x^{2} y' \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 6 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(2 x e^{y}\\right)&=2 x y' e^{y} + 2 e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the equation, collect all terms containing $y^{\\prime}$, and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + y' \\left(3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 6 y\\right)&=2 x y' e^{y}\\\\\n&\\quad +2 e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 x e^{y} + 6 y\\right)y^{\\prime}&=- 6 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad +2 e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide by the coefficient of $y^{\\prime}$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{- 6 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 e^{y}}{3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 x e^{y} + 6 y}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies where $3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 x e^{y} + 6 y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711374 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(1,-1)$ for the curve $- x^{2} + y e^{- x y}=- e - 1$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The outer product and the exponential chain rule both matter. First differentiate the exponential input:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(- x y\\right)&=- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{- x y}\\right)&=\\left(- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\right) e^{- x y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(- x^{2} + y e^{- x y}\\right)=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(- e - 1\\right)\\\\\n&\\frac{dy}{dx} e^{- x y} - 2 x + y \\left(- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\right) e^{- x y}=0\\\\\n&\\left(- x y e^{- x y} + e^{- x y}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\left(- 2 x - y^{2} e^{- x y}\\right)\\\\\n&\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{- 2 x - y^{2} e^{- x y}}{- x y e^{- x y} + e^{- x y}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(1,-1)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $2 e\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(1,-1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{- e - 2}{2 e}\\\\\n&=e^{-1} + \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711375 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(1,-1)$ for the curve $x e^{- x y} - y^{2}=-1 + e$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The outer product and the exponential chain rule both matter. First differentiate the exponential input:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(- x y\\right)&=- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{- x y}\\right)&=\\left(- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\right) e^{- x y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(x e^{- x y} - y^{2}\\right)=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(-1 + e\\right)\\\\\n&- 2 \\frac{dy}{dx} y + x \\left(- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\right) e^{- x y} + e^{- x y}=0\\\\\n&\\left(- x^{2} e^{- x y} - 2 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\left(- x y e^{- x y} + e^{- x y}\\right)\\\\\n&\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{- x y e^{- x y} + e^{- x y}}{- x^{2} e^{- x y} - 2 y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(1,-1)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $2 - e\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(1,-1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2 e}{2 - e}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 e}{-2 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711380 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(-1,1)$ for the curve $x^{2} + 2 y e^{- x y}=1 + 2 e$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The outer product and the exponential chain rule both matter. First differentiate the exponential input:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(- x y\\right)&=- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{- x y}\\right)&=\\left(- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\right) e^{- x y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y e^{- x y}\\right)=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(1 + 2 e\\right)\\\\\n&2 \\frac{dy}{dx} e^{- x y} + 2 x + 2 y \\left(- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\right) e^{- x y}=0\\\\\n&\\left(- 2 x y e^{- x y} + 2 e^{- x y}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\left(2 x - 2 y^{2} e^{- x y}\\right)\\\\\n&\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2 x - 2 y^{2} e^{- x y}}{- 2 x y e^{- x y} + 2 e^{- x y}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(-1,1)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $4 e\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(-1,1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{- 2 e - 2}{4 e}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1 + e}{2 e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711383 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(-1,-1)$ for the curve $- 2 x e^{- x y} + 2 y^{2}=\\frac{2}{e} + 2$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The outer product and the exponential chain rule both matter. First differentiate the exponential input:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(- x y\\right)&=- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(e^{- x y}\\right)&=\\left(- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\right) e^{- x y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(- 2 x e^{- x y} + 2 y^{2}\\right)=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{2}{e} + 2\\right)\\\\\n&4 \\frac{dy}{dx} y - 2 x \\left(- \\frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\right) e^{- x y} - 2 e^{- x y}=0\\\\\n&\\left(2 x^{2} e^{- x y} + 4 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\left(2 x y e^{- x y} - 2 e^{- x y}\\right)\\\\\n&\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2 x y e^{- x y} - 2 e^{- x y}}{2 x^{2} e^{- x y} + 4 y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(-1,-1)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $-4 + \\frac{2}{e}\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(-1,-1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{0}{-4 + \\frac{2}{e}}\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711386 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(-3,-3)$ for the curve $2 x^{3} - y^{3}=-27$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule and the chain rule, treating $y$ as a function of $x$.\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(2 x^{3} - y^{3}\\right)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(-27\\right)\\\\\n- 3 \\frac{dy}{dx} y^{2} + 6 x^{2}&=0\\\\\n\\left(- 3 y^{2}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(6 x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{6 x^{2}}{- 3 y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(-3,-3)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $-27\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(-3,-3)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{54}{-27}\\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711395 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,1)$ for the curve $2 x^{3} - 3 y^{3}=-5$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule and the chain rule, treating $y$ as a function of $x$.\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(2 x^{3} - 3 y^{3}\\right)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(-5\\right)\\\\\n- 9 \\frac{dy}{dx} y^{2} + 6 x^{2}&=0\\\\\n\\left(- 9 y^{2}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(6 x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{6 x^{2}}{- 9 y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(-1,1)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $-9\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(-1,1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{6}{-9}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711396 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,-1)$ for the curve $x^{3} - 3 y^{3}=4$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the power rule and the chain rule, treating $y$ as a function of $x$.\n\nDifferentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(x^{3} - 3 y^{3}\\right)&=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n- 9 \\frac{dy}{dx} y^{2} + 3 x^{2}&=0\\\\\n\\left(- 9 y^{2}\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(3 x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{3 x^{2}}{- 9 y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(1,-1)$, the coefficient of $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $-9\\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm&=\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(1,-1)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{-9}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711405 :question/problem "Given that $y^{2} + 2 y-(3 \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)})=0$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. The chain rule gives $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(3 \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)})=9 \\sec^{2}{\\left(3 x \\right)}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(y^{2} + 2 y\\right)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n2 y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=9 \\sec^{2}{\\left(3 x \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 y + 2\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=9 \\sec^{2}{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{9 \\sec^{2}{\\left(3 x \\right)}}{2 y + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies on real branches where $2 y + 2\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711414 :question/problem "Given that $y^{3} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}-(3 x^{2})=3$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. Apply the chain rule to both functions of $y$, including the trigonometric derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(y^{3} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{2} + 3\\right)\\\\\n3 y^{2}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=6 x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(3 y^{2} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=6 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{6 x}{3 y^{2} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies on real branches where $3 y^{2} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711421 :question/problem "Given that $3^{y} - y-(2 x^{2})=0$, calculate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a function $y(x)$. The exponential chain rule gives $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(3^{y})=3^{y} \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3^{y} - y\\right)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(2 x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n3^{y} \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}-1\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=4 x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the terms containing $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$, factor, and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(3^{y} \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - 1\\right)\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=4 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{4 x}{3^{y} \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula applies on real branches where $3^{y} \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - 1\\ne 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711713 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i-x\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t)=\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i+\\cos t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\le t\\le\\frac\\pi2$." :question/worked-solution "Use\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nAlong the path, $x=\\sin t$ and $y=\\cos t$, so\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i-\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\nDifferentiate the supplied path:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i-\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\nThen\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\n&=\\cos^2t+(-\\sin t)(-\\sin t)\\\\\n&=\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}1\\,\\mathrm dt\n=\\left[t\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}=\\frac\\pi2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711719 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y)=x\\,\\mathbf i+(y-1)\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i+t\\cos t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\le t\\le\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrized line-integral formula:\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=\\int_0^\\pi\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nSubstitute $x=t\\sin t$ and $y=t\\cos t$:\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\n=t\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i+(t\\cos t-1)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\nBoth coordinates require the product rule:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=(\\sin t+t\\cos t)\\,\\mathbf i\n+(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\nTake the dot product and expand:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\n&=t\\sin t(\\sin t+t\\cos t)\\\\\n&\\quad +(t\\cos t-1)(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\\\\n&=t\\sin^2t+t^2\\sin t\\cos t\\\\\n&\\quad +t\\cos^2t-t^2\\sin t\\cos t\\\\\n&\\quad -\\cos t+t\\sin t\\\\\n&=t-\\cos t+t\\sin t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe mixed terms cancel, but the offset in the field leaves two extra terms. To integrate them, note that\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(-t\\cos t)=-\\cos t+t\\sin t.\n$$\nThus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=\\int_0^\\pi(t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^2}{2}-t\\cos t\\right]_0^\\pi\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi^2}{2}-\\pi(-1)-0\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi^2}{2}+\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711731 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i+(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is given by $\\mathbf r(t)=(1+2t)\\,\\mathbf i+(2-t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\le t\\le1$.\n\n*Hint: Use*\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=\\int_0^1\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt,\n\\qquad \\mathbf r'(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Along the path, $x=1+2t$ and $y=2-t$. Substitute both coordinates into the field:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\n&=(2-t)\\,\\mathbf i+((1+2t)+(2-t))\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\n&=(2-t)\\,\\mathbf i+(3+t)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nDifferentiating the path gives $\\mathbf r'(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j$. Take the dot product, keeping the negative sign of the second tangent component:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\n&=2(2-t)-(3+t)\\\\\n&=1-3t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow evaluate the definite integral:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n&=\\int_0^1(1-3t)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[t-\\frac32t^2\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=1-\\frac32=-\\frac12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711747 :question/problem "The curve has equation $4 x^{2} + 6 x + 2 y^{2} + 2 y=42$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(-3,-4\\right)$ meets the $x$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nGive exact values.\n\n$P=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 x + 6+\\left(4 y + 2\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(4 y + 2\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(8 x + 6\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{8 x + 6}{4 y + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{-18}{-14}=- \\frac{9}{7}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=- \\frac{9}{7}$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-4\\right)&=- \\frac{9}{7}\\left(x-\\left(-3\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=- \\frac{9}{7}\\left(x-\\left(-3\\right)\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=- \\frac{9}{7}\\left(x-\\left(-3\\right)\\right)+\\left(-4\\right) \\\\\nx-\\left(-3\\right)&=-\\frac{-4}{- \\frac{9}{7}} \\\\\nx&=- \\frac{55}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(- \\frac{55}{9},0\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711749 :question/problem "The curve has equation $x^{2} + 4 x - 4 y=25$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(3,-1\\right)$ meets the $y$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nGive exact values.\n\n$P=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 x + 4+\\left(-4\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(-4\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(2 x + 4\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{2 x + 4}{-4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{10}{-4}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=\\frac{5}{2}$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-1\\right)&=\\frac{5}{2}\\left(x-\\left(3\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=\\frac{5}{2}\\left(x-\\left(3\\right)\\right)+\\left(-1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{5}{2}\\left(0-\\left(3\\right)\\right)+\\left(-1\\right) \\\\\ny&=- \\frac{17}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(0,- \\frac{17}{2}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711753 :question/problem "The curve has equation $x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=24$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(-4,-2\\right)$ meets the $y$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nGive exact values.\n\n$P=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 x+\\left(4 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(4 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{2 x}{4 y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{-8}{-8}=-1\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=-1$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-2\\right)&=-1\\left(x-\\left(-4\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=-1\\left(x-\\left(-4\\right)\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=-1\\left(0-\\left(-4\\right)\\right)+\\left(-2\\right) \\\\\ny&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(0,-6\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711758 :question/problem "The curve has equation $x y + x - 2 y=8$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(-4,-2\\right)$ meets the $x$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nGive exact values.\n\n$P=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The product rule gives $\\frac{d}{dx}(xy)=y+x\\frac{dy}{dx}$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny + 1+\\left(x - 2\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(x - 2\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(y + 1\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{y + 1}{x - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{-1}{-6}=- \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=- \\frac{1}{6}$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-2\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{6}\\left(x-\\left(-4\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=- \\frac{1}{6}\\left(x-\\left(-4\\right)\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=- \\frac{1}{6}\\left(x-\\left(-4\\right)\\right)+\\left(-2\\right) \\\\\nx-\\left(-4\\right)&=-\\frac{-2}{- \\frac{1}{6}} \\\\\nx&=-16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(-16,0\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711763 :question/problem "The curve has equation $x y + 2 x + 2 y=14$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(1,4\\right)$ meets the $x$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nGive exact values.\n\n$P=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The product rule gives $\\frac{d}{dx}(xy)=y+x\\frac{dy}{dx}$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny + 2+\\left(x + 2\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(x + 2\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(y + 2\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{y + 2}{x + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{6}{3}=-2\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=-2$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(4\\right)&=-2\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=-2\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=-2\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)+\\left(4\\right) \\\\\nx-\\left(1\\right)&=-\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\\nx&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(3,0\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711789 :question/problem "The curve has equation $4 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}=18$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(-2,1\\right)$ meets the $y$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nUse $y-y_0=m(x-x_0)$ with the given point. On the $y$-axis, $x=0$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 x+\\left(4 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(4 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(8 x\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{8 x}{4 y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{-16}{4}=4\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=4$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(1\\right)&=4\\left(x-\\left(-2\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=4\\left(x-\\left(-2\\right)\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\left(0-\\left(-2\\right)\\right)+\\left(1\\right) \\\\\ny&=9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(0,9\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711799 :question/problem "The curve has equation $2 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=72$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(2,-4\\right)$ meets the $x$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nUse $y-y_0=m(x-x_0)$ with the given point. On the $x$-axis, $y=0$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 x+\\left(8 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(8 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(4 x\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{4 x}{8 y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{8}{-32}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-4\\right)&=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)+\\left(-4\\right) \\\\\nx-\\left(2\\right)&=-\\frac{-4}{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\nx&=18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(18,0\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711810 :question/problem "The curve has equation $x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=68$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(2,-4\\right)$ meets the $y$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nUse $y-y_0=m(x-x_0)$ with the given point. On the $y$-axis, $x=0$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 x+\\left(8 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(8 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{2 x}{8 y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{4}{-32}=\\frac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=\\frac{1}{8}$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-4\\right)&=\\frac{1}{8}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=\\frac{1}{8}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{1}{8}\\left(0-\\left(2\\right)\\right)+\\left(-4\\right) \\\\\ny&=- \\frac{17}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(0,- \\frac{17}{4}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186711812 :question/problem "The curve has equation $3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}=30$. The **tangent** line at $\\left(-3,1\\right)$ meets the $x$-axis at $P$. Find the coordinates of $P$.\n\nUse $y-y_0=m(x-x_0)$ with the given point. On the $x$-axis, $y=0$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 x+\\left(6 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\\\\n\\left(6 y\\right)\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\left(6 x\\right) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\\frac{6 x}{6 y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the given point:\n\n$$\nm_{\\mathrm{t}}=-\\frac{-18}{6}=3\n$$\n\nThe tangent line uses $m=3$.\n\nUse the given point and this slope:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(1\\right)&=3\\left(x-\\left(-3\\right)\\right) \\\\\ny&=3\\left(x-\\left(-3\\right)\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=3\\left(x-\\left(-3\\right)\\right)+\\left(1\\right) \\\\\nx-\\left(-3\\right)&=-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\\nx&=- \\frac{10}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $P=\\left(- \\frac{10}{3},0\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712187 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf g(t)=\\langle t\\cos t,\\,1+t\\sin t\\rangle\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\pi/2$? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n![The planar spiral segment with its parameter endpoints and direction of travel.](images/68/689af73f5d8a1f08682e5e56feefbbeb8337ee28cd352815af43c6c8cb32c369.png)" :question/worked-solution "Apply the product rule in both components; the constant $1$ differentiates to zero:\n$$\n\\mathbf g'(t)=\\langle\\cos t-t\\sin t,\\,\\sin t+t\\cos t\\rangle.\n$$\nSquare the two components and add:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|^2\n&=(\\cos t-t\\sin t)^2+(\\sin t+t\\cos t)^2\\\\\n&=\\cos^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t\\\\\n&\\quad+\\sin^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\\\\n&=(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)+t^2(\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t)\\\\\n&=1+t^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe mixed terms cancel. Taking the nonnegative square root and retaining the parameter bounds, we obtain\n$$\nL=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712188 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the spatial curve\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t)=\\langle e^t\\cos(2t),\\,e^t\\sin(2t),\\,t e^t\\rangle\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n![The spatial curve with its parameter endpoints and direction of travel.](images/51/51cf2056fa2270172cefa8d198a600d0486fb884605c93521c9df7c614846e87.png)" :question/worked-solution "Use the product rule in all three components and the chain rule for the angle $2t$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x'(t)&=e^t(\\cos(2t)-2\\sin(2t)),\\\\\nf_y'(t)&=e^t(\\sin(2t)+2\\cos(2t)),\\\\\nf_z'(t)&=e^t(t+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTo simplify its magnitude, first expand the two trigonometric squares:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(\\cos(2t)-2\\sin(2t))^2+(\\sin(2t)+2\\cos(2t))^2\\\\\n&\\quad=\\cos^2(2t)-4\\sin(2t)\\cos(2t)+4\\sin^2(2t)\\\\\n&\\qquad+\\sin^2(2t)+4\\sin(2t)\\cos(2t)+4\\cos^2(2t)\\\\\n&\\quad=5(\\sin^2(2t)+\\cos^2(2t))=5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nInclude the third derivative component when forming the speed:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\n&=\\sqrt{e^{2t}\\bigl(5+(t+1)^2\\bigr)}\\\\\n&=e^t\\sqrt{t^2+2t+6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $\\sqrt{e^{2t}}=e^t$ because $e^t>0$. Therefore,\n$$\nL=\\int_0^1\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n=\\int_0^1 e^t\\sqrt{t^2+2t+6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712189 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin(3t),\\,\\cos(2t)\\rangle\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\pi/4$? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n![The planar curve with its parameter endpoints and direction of travel.](images/39/39f59b0f1d6f6df41d552f082b3b05c186f934b39cfd87da8e6208d48f66f989.png)" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each component, including the inner factors $3$ and $2$:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle3\\cos(3t),\\,-2\\sin(2t)\\rangle.\n$$\nThe speed is the magnitude of this derivative:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(3\\cos(3t))^2+(-2\\sin(2t))^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{9\\cos^2(3t)+4\\sin^2(2t)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBoth derivative components are squared, so the negative sign in the second component contributes a positive term. Retaining the given parameter bounds gives\n$$\nL=\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n=\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sqrt{9\\cos^2(3t)+4\\sin^2(2t)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712197 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=x\\,\\mathbf i+z\\,\\mathbf j+y\\,\\mathbf k$ and $C$ is the path\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=-\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i+\\sec t\\,\\mathbf j+\\cos t\\,\\mathbf k,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le\\frac\\pi4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nAlong the curve, $x=-\\sin t$, $y=\\sec t$, and $z=\\cos t$. Therefore\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=-\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i+\\cos t\\,\\mathbf j+\\sec t\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\nDifferentiate each curve component, keeping both negative signs:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=-\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+\\sec t\\tan t\\,\\mathbf j-\\sin t\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\nTake the dot product before integrating:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)&=(-\\sin t)(-\\cos t)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\cos t\\sec t\\tan t-\\sec t\\sin t\\\\\n&=\\sin t\\cos t+\\tan t-\\tan t\\\\\n&=\\sin t\\cos t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe second and third terms cancel since $\\cos t\\sec t=1$ and $\\sec t\\sin t=\\tan t$. Finally,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sin t\\cos t\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac12\\sin^2t\\right]_0^{\\pi/4}\\\\\n&=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)^2-0=\\frac14.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712207 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=y\\,\\mathbf i+z\\,\\mathbf j+x\\,\\mathbf k$ and $C$ is the path\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^1\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nAlong the curve, $x=t$, $y=t^2$, and $z=t^3$. Hence\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=t^2\\,\\mathbf i+t^3\\,\\mathbf j+t\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\nDifferentiating the curve gives\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i+2t\\,\\mathbf j+3t^2\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\nMatch corresponding vector components in the dot product:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)&=t^2\\cdot1+t^3\\cdot2t+t\\cdot3t^2\\\\\n&=t^2+2t^4+3t^3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_0^1(t^2+2t^4+3t^3)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^3}{3}+\\frac{2t^5}{5}+\\frac{3t^4}{4}\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\frac13+\\frac25+\\frac34=\\frac{89}{60}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712210 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=x\\,\\mathbf i+z\\,\\mathbf j+y\\,\\mathbf k$ and\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+2t\\,\\mathbf j+(1-t)\\,\\mathbf k,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\n*Hint: Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^1\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$ with $\\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$.*" :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrized line-integral formula:\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^1\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\nAlong the curve, $x=t$, $y=2t$, and $z=1-t$. Substitute these coordinates into the corresponding field components:\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=t\\,\\mathbf i+(1-t)\\,\\mathbf j+2t\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\nDifferentiate the curve component by component:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k.\n$$\nThe dot product includes the negative third component:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)&=t\\cdot1+(1-t)\\cdot2+2t\\cdot(-1)\\\\\n&=2-3t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFinally,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_0^1(2-3t)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[2t-\\frac32t^2\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=2-\\frac32=\\frac12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712222 :question/problem "![The specified curve segment is blue; its continuation is red.](images/e1/e1986870b945ca2f5337b1d33b1d911af9e73cf2369876a8d92cea2aa0e8e067.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^3-3t,\\,3t^2\\rangle\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$." :question/worked-solution "First, differentiate each component and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 3t^2-3,\\,6t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(3t^2-3)^2+(6t)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{9t^4-18t^2+9+36t^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{9(t^2+1)^2}\\\\\n&=3(t^2+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last step uses $t^2+1>0$. Integrate the speed over the given parameter interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL&=\\int_0^1\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 3(t^2+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[t^3+3t\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712232 :question/problem "![The specified curve segment is blue; its continuation is red.](images/85/8502d80ae296e43f03f417781d54ce777f34eb34fef69db4df0bf09119d14d39.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 7-2t^3,\\,3t^2+10\\rangle\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=\\sqrt{3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, differentiate each component and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle -6t^2,\\,6t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{36t^4+36t^2}\\\\\n&=6\\sqrt{t^2(t^2+1)}\\\\\n&=6|t|\\sqrt{t^2+1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSpeed is nonnegative. Since $|t|=-t$ on $[-1,0]$ and $|t|=t$ on $[0,\\sqrt{3}]$, split the integral at $t=0$:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{-1}^0 -6t\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n  +\\int_0^{\\sqrt{3}}6t\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor each integral use $u=t^2+1$, with $\\mathrm{d}u=2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. On the first interval $u$ decreases from $2$ to $1$; on the second it increases from $1$ to $4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL&=-3\\int_2^1 u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u+3\\int_1^4 u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=-\\left[2u^{3/2}\\right]_2^1+\\left[2u^{3/2}\\right]_1^4\\\\\n&=(4\\sqrt{2}-2)+(16-2)\\\\\n&=12+4\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth pieces contribute positive length. Replacing $|t|$ by $t$ on the whole interval would subtract the first piece instead of adding it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712236 :question/problem "![The specified curve segment is blue; its continuation is red.](images/d4/d4f2c2141b391c380e44a40f17ba537bfb1b9ce4b26acb6ef8d38fb3bdd3421c.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 7-2t^3,\\,3t^2+10\\rangle\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$." :question/worked-solution "First, differentiate each component and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle -6t^2,\\,6t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{36t^4+36t^2}\\\\\n&=6|t|\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\\\\n&=6t\\sqrt{t^2+1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $t\\geq0$ on $[0,1]$, so $|t|=t$. Thus\n\n$$\nL=\\int_0^1 6t\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nUse $u=t^2+1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The bounds become $u=1$ and $u=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL&=3\\int_1^2 u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\left[2u^{3/2}\\right]_1^2\\\\\n&=2(2\\sqrt{2}-1)\\\\\n&=4\\sqrt{2}-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
